# Zig lineage 2 — maintenance ABI v2 handoff

## Delivered state

Maintenance submission before evaluator feedback. `service` is a Linux AArch64
ELF built with pinned Zig 0.16.0 in ReleaseSafe mode. It dynamically links the
supplied `/adapter/librosetta.so`, designated `librocksdb.so.7.8`, and libc.
The service imports only `rn_verify_v2`, not `rn_verify_v1`. No kernel changes,
group commit, service test hooks, or durability relaxation were introduced.

Files:

- `build.sh`: unchanged executable build recipe and dynamic library paths.
- `main.zig`: v2 arena offsets/bitmap, explicit work priority, recovery integration.
- `scheduler.zig`: two intrusive FIFO queues and a shared H,H,H,N selection cycle;
  also contains two standalone unit tests excluded from the service executable.
- `storage.zig`: unchanged predecessor RocksDB wrapper and native diagnostics.
- `reuse/storage.zig`: unchanged supplied infrastructure.
- `tests/check.py`: 18 black-box cases, direct v1/v2 native comparison, independent
  RocksDB inspection after the writer stops, crash/recovery and upgrade tests.
- `EXPERIMENTS.md`: initial history plus maintenance attempts and evidence limits.
- `SUBMISSION.txt`, `SHA256SUMS`: phase timing and delivered artifact hashes.

The predecessor source, executable, tests, documents and manifest were preserved
before editing under `tmp/maintenance-baseline/`. That directory is evidence,
not a runtime dependency. No other lineage or evaluator was inspected.

Build and verify exclusively through the broker:

```sh
python3 run.py './build.sh' 120
python3 run.py '/opt/zig/zig test scheduler.zig -O ReleaseSafe' 120
python3 run.py 'PREDECESSOR_SERVICE=/workspace/tmp/maintenance-baseline/service python3 tests/check.py ./service' 180
python3 run.py 'python3 public_check.py ./service' 120
```

Without `PREDECESSOR_SERVICE`, the black-box suite explicitly skips the actual
predecessor-executable upgrade case; seeded compatibility/recovery cases still
run. Launch inside the broker as `./service DATADIR SOCKET_PATH`. DATADIR is
created recursively. RocksDB owns the exclusive writer lock. SOCKET_PATH must
be fresh; the launcher removes stale paths after crashes. Successful draining
shutdown closes the database and exits zero.

## Native execution and scheduling

Four persistent worker threads share the pool mutex, condition and completion
queue. Each `Work` owns its raw bytes, parsed item metadata, and allocation arena
until its worker has returned. Workers create a native arena, construct v2 item
offsets relative to that arena, call `rn_verify_v2`, and destroy the native arena
only after return. A u64 bitmap is checked against all per-item success statuses,
including bit 63. Inconsistent bitmap or a nonzero batch return is an execution
failure. Per-item status and result wire fields retain v1 encodings.

Priority defaults to normal; explicit high and normal are accepted for submit
and evaluate. Admission persists the selected priority. Priority is excluded
from the digest, so a retry cannot alter an existing job's priority.

The scheduler's pop under the pool mutex is the dispatch decision. With both
queues nonempty, selections repeat high, high, high, normal. A single nonempty
queue drains without advancing the cycle. FIFO order within each priority is
preserved. One cycle is shared by all four workers, starts with high, and resets
on process restart. The complete recovered backlog is loaded in sequence order
into these queues before any worker starts.

Evaluate shares these queues and the four slots but is nonpersistent. Cancelled
work remains owned and queued until selected; a selected already-cancelled job
can skip native execution, and that selection consumes its normal scheduler turn.
Cancellation never frees input still reachable by a worker. Native entry and
completion may be reordered by concurrently running threads after selection;
terminal database writes remain strictly admission ordered.

## Durability and compatibility

Schema stays 1. Recovery accepts v1 pending rows with normal priority and v2 rows
with either valid priority. Existing receipts, ID indexes, cancellation intents,
admission sequence, checkpoint and accounting are preserved. There is no rebuild
or repair path. Unknown schema, malformed priority or inconsistent rows fail
before socket exposure. All rows are validated before native execution starts.

The coordinator remains the sole owner of admission, cancellation, terminal
transitions, accounting, connections and pending slots. Each admission is one
sync/WAL WriteBatch with the complete pending payload, ID index, sequence and
accounting. Each terminal receipt is one sync/WAL WriteBatch removing pending
and cancellation rows and advancing the contiguous checkpoint/accounting. Receipt
retention and ID indexes are unlimited for this bounded trial.

Cancellation intent is durable before `ok`. Repeated pending cancellation is
idempotent. The coordinator serializes the intent snapshot and terminal C write
entry; a completed cancellation before that boundary is honored. Requests
processed after terminal commitment receive `too_late`. A native allocation or
execution failure on acknowledged work stops diagnostically without terminalizing
it. Evaluate returns `execution_failure`. Storage errors stop without acknowledging
the failed transition. No further writes follow an uncertain write result.

Admission response is queued before dispatch; terminal events follow durable
receipt. Client tokens prevent descriptor reuse from redirecting replies.
Disconnect does not cancel. Existing per-client request order, eight-client
limit, output backpressure, 1,024-job/64-MiB admission bounds and shutdown behavior
are unchanged. Nonterminal accounting includes completed-but-uncommitted work.

## Validation and evidence limits

First maintenance build passed. Both scheduler tests passed. All 18 black-box
cases passed (4.378 seconds reported by unittest), including the actual preserved
v1 executable's datadir. Public smoke and Zig formatting checks passed. `readelf`
confirmed AArch64, dynamic adapter/RocksDB dependencies and only v2 verification
imports. Full test artifacts: `/workspace/tmp/zig-v2-tests-7cse70mp`.

New evidence includes success bit 63/all/none/mixed cases, offset equivalence to
v1, high-priority evaluate, immutable retry priority, pending status priority,
mixed-priority process crashes and durable cancellation, mixed-priority recovery,
invalid disk-priority rejection without repair, and byte-for-byte old receipt
retention. The real-v1 upgrade case crashed the old executable with outstanding
normal-priority work and added a valid cancellation fixture while its writer was
stopped, then recovered with v2. Other tests exercise acknowledged cancellation.

The scheduler tests cover repeated HHHN turns and every cycle position across
empty/single-queue intervals for both priorities. They do not establish an
externally observed native-entry ordering under all thread interleavings. No
native entry/write barrier interposer, service timing hook, ASan, whole-service
race detector, allocator failure injection or storage-I/O failure injection was
added. Four native worker slots exist by construction; exact externally held
concurrency/cancellation-boundary qualification remains unclaimed. Process-crash
tests do not prove power-loss durability. No performance comparison is claimed.

## Ambiguities and decisions

The supplied contracts do not explicitly specify evaluate participation in the
priority cycle or cycle consumption by skipped cancelled jobs; the choices above
make both deterministic at queue selection. The adapter header does not describe
bitmap bit ordering; direct v1/v2 comparisons support bit i corresponding to item
i and zero bits outside the batch. Wire output adds no bitmap field.

Existing wire choices remain: unknown JSON fields reserved/ignored, known fields
strictly typed, duplicate keys rejected; empty/overlarge batches invalid; retry
returns existing plus sequence; both submit/evaluate return draining during drain;
JSON cap excludes newline; unrecoverable correlations use the empty string.

The freeze manifest/gate evidence is absent and preparation prose remains in the
contracts. The user's explicit maintenance implementation instruction authorized
this phase. No gate, evaluator qualification or external evidence is claimed.
